# Block32 preregistration amendment — selector copy before uncompute

## Status

This amendment is frozen before the target runner is source-pinned or used for
science inference.  It corrects the operator order in `GOAL.md`; every other
scope and terminal rule remains in force.

## Preexecution defect found

The originally displayed active Kraus word put the ten-step handoff and reached
second pair use before `U^dagger`.  A literal support intersection found that
this order is not valid on the complete route family.  In four canonical
opposite-exit plans, some reached second-use target Blocks contain STATUS sites
sampled by the Block31 comparator.  A second-use writer can therefore change a
sample before the inverse comparator runs.  Disjoint transient work alone is
not enough to justify cleanup.

No target execution or result preceded this finding.

## Corrected active word

After `U`, the complete active projector validates the full Block28 pointer
configuration, matching one-hot route token, clean comparator domain, Blank
persistent selector, and complete declared `158`-Blank carrier.  The route
token is first copied into the disjoint persistent selector.  The comparator is
then reversed while every sampled first-use pointer bit is still unchanged.
Only after this cleanup does the selector-controlled route and reached pair
instrument write its targets:

\[
 L_{r,\alpha}=R_{r,\alpha}\,U^\dagger W_rP_rU,
 \qquad
 L_{\rm STOP}=U^\dagger(I-P_A)U,
 \qquad P_A=\sum_rP_r.
\]

Here operators act from right to left.  `W_r` changes only the separate
selector bank, so it commutes with the comparator inverse.  `R_{r,alpha}` is
controlled by that persistent route selector together with the exact QND
source Records.  The ten route writes and selected two second-use writes then
consume twelve Blanks, leaving `146`; all eight possible second-use target
Blocks had to be Blank in the pre-action carrier.

